 @dirtyvinyl8817  What does it say about nVidia that they hold quite a bit of responsibility for Vista having a bad reputation with stability? nVidia's code caused more problems than Microsoft's own code for 2007 with over a quarter of the total submitted crash reports being attributed to their driver crashing: https://www.theregister.com/2008/03/28/nvidia_vista_drivers/ 

Then there's that driver that basically fried GPUs by accidentally disabling the fan during high load around thanks to a faulty fan profile, the GTX 590 having VRMs catching fire on a semi-regular basis, bumpgate where they tried to pass off the costs of an internal production issue onto laptop makers, the nVidia Linux drivers breaking every few months or the many, many, many crashes people have had with nVidia's extra tools over the years. Turns out nVidia has had loads and loads of driver issues over the years, just like AMD/ATi has.


Fact is, both companies have had shitty times with their drivers and the extra cruft that comes with it. rDNA1 had a shit time, but it seems by and large fixed nor does it really say much for the rDNA2 GPUs...Just like nVidia's Vista drivers absolutely were bad enough that I dropped from a 6800GS to a x1600Pro for stabilities sake, but that didn't prevent them from fixing those issues over 2007-2008 and having some great years with GT200 and Fermi. Funnily enough, both companies tend to try and fix their weakest points with each release.

